' Notes are capped at 5 lines x 50 columns; one idea per anchor.
' This file grows as the delivery plan's teardown slices land. Landed so far:
' T2 (step timing), T1 (deadlines), T3 (teardown as a value), T4 (signals in
' teardown) and T6 (the live step line), all 2026-11-10.
'
' ── EVERY STEP LEAVES ONE LINE IN THE RUN LOG (T2) ─────────────────────────
' Before this, the run log stamped section headers only. A finished run could
' not say how long any teardown step took, so "typical" silence was unknown
' and only the ceilings were (terminal-handoff-probes.puml, C2).
' `step(name, fn)` runs fn and records its name, duration and outcome.
' `stepDo` is the same for a call that returns no error. The record goes to
' the run log through ui.Logf, as
'   step: <name> <duration> ok
'   step: <name> <duration> failed: <first line of the error>
' THE ROW NEVER REACHES THE TERMINAL. ui.Logf writes to the log's own printer and
' is a no-op when no log is open, so a run whose log failed to open records
' nothing; it does not fail.
' THE NAMES ARE CONSTANTS in teardown_steps.go, because the step list that
' later draws them (delivery plan, T6) reads the same names.
'   agent              the agent's own run, start to exit
'   stop wait          from the Ctrl+C dialog's remove answer to the sbx
'                      client exiting (bounded by stopWait, 30s)
'   credential check · clone mode · pushed branches · egress record ·
'   memory evidence    the five calls that open teardown
'   clone              the whole carry; its parts are logged under it:
'     clone/.env · clone/snapshot · clone/output · clone/branches · clone/spec
'   resume state · home config · remove sandbox
' A PARENT AND ITS PARTS ARE BOTH LOGGED. `clone` is the total; the parts add
' up to less than it, because the probes between them (`sbx ls`, the clone-mode
' probe inside PreserveClone) belong to the parent. A clone run starts up to 31
' subprocesses; one row per subprocess would bury the steps an operator names.
' PreserveClone also serves the retire path at run START, so a retired
' sandbox's carry logs the same `clone/…` rows there. That is why the prefix
' is `step:` and not `teardown:`.
' THE SINK IS AN ATOMIC POINTER. Tests swap it to read the steps; one test in
' the package runs in parallel and reaches carryClone.
'
' ── FOUR CALLS HAD NO DEADLINE; NOW EACH HAS ONE (T1) ──────────────────────
' kit-lifecycle.puml bounded the carry path on 2026-10-22 and left SaveState
' and teardown's `sbx rm` STILL OPEN. Two more were found unbounded on
' 2026-11-10: sandboxReflog and the pushed-branch `git fetch`.
' THE REFLOG READ RAN BEFORE THE CARRY. sbx.Exists answers true when `sbx ls`
' fails, so on a wedged daemon a clone run waited 10s for `sbx ls` and then
' started `sbx exec … git log -g` with no deadline. Teardown stopped there,
' printed nothing, and no work had reached home.
' All four now run through boundCommand / guestRun under guestExecDeadline
' (3m, the ceiling the Executor chose on 2026-10-22: "36s probe 3m others").
'   sandboxReflog           a timeout reads as "no reflog": no pushed-branch
'                           lines and no question
'   the pushed `git fetch`  a timeout prints "⚠ <branch> — not fetched" with
'                           the deadline dim under it (R2)
'   SaveState               teardown and retire pass guestRun; a timeout
'                           prints "⚠ resume state — not saved" (R2)
'   `sbx rm --force`        removeSandbox; a timeout keeps the sandbox, and
'                           the closing line's dim fix is the rescue line:
'                           check `sbx ls`, then `sbx rm --force <name>` once
'                           sbx answers (R2: removeSandbox prints nothing)
' NEVER AN AUTOMATIC SHIM KILL: killing the shim made the daemon respawn it and
' stall again (kit-lifecycle.puml).
' A REMOVAL THAT TIMES OUT MAY BE HALF-DONE. The rescue line says to look
' first, because the sandbox's state is unknown at that point.
' [Unverified] Whether 3m is generous or tight for a healthy `sbx rm` or state
' save. No finished run had per-step times before T2; the first logs with
' `step:` rows answer it.
' Nine host-local git and tar calls had no deadline after T1. T3 gave each
' one (below).
'
' ── TEARDOWN IS A VALUE, CALLED BEFORE THE RESULT (T3) ─────────────────────
' Until T3 teardown was a deferred closure at the end of Run. Three things
' followed from that shape:
'   it ran AFTER Run chose its result, so nothing teardown learned could
'     change the exit status (T4 returns 130 when the operator leaves);
'   it captured Run's locals, so no test could call it;
'   a panic in one step unwound past every later step, the removal included.
' `teardown` (teardown.go) holds what the closure captured: the Input, the
' RunConfig, the secrets, how the run ended, its two timestamps, the agent's
' last output and the home-access commit. Run builds it and calls
' `td.run(ctx)` on the line before it computes the result.
' THE PLAN IS DATA. `teardownPlan(failed)` lists an ending's top-level steps
' in the order they run:
'   credential check · clone mode · pushed branches · egress record ·
'   memory evidence · clone · resume state · home config
'   + remove sandbox, when the run did not fail
' A failed run keeps the sandbox for diagnosis, so its plan has no removal.
' T6's step list (W5) draws "step n of N" from the same function.
' THE PLAN IS AN UPPER BOUND ON A NORMAL EXIT. Two endings stop before the
' removal: work stranded in the sandbox, and an OpenCode engine that holds
' the history. Both keep the sandbox and say so.
' ONE CONTEXT REACHES EVERY SUBPROCESS. Input.Stop carries it, so a function
' that already takes the Input gained no parameter. boundCommand derives each
' call's deadline from it: a call ends at its own ceiling OR when the context
' ends, and its error says which ("gave no answer within 3m0s", or "stopped").
' The nine calls T1 left open run under it at guestExecDeadline: the host
' reflog read, the spec restore, gitPaths, the remote fetch, the bundle fetch,
' hostTips, the two calls in reportCloneRefs, and the lift's `tar`.
' Until T4 passes a context a signal ends, Run passes context.Background().
' A STEP THAT WOULD START AFTER THE CONTEXT ENDED IS SKIPPED, and logged as
'   step: <name> 0s failed: skipped: teardown was stopped
' On a normal exit a skipped carry reads as stranded work, so the sandbox
' stays and its removal command prints. A stopped teardown never removes.
' A PANIC IS THE STEP'S ERROR. `do` recovers it, records `failed: panic: …`
' and returns it. The step's own warning prints and the next step runs.
' [Unverified] No panic in teardown was ever observed. The recover exists
' because one would leave the agent's work in a sandbox with no line saying so.
' WHAT T3 DID NOT CHANGE: the order, the lines printed, and which ending
' keeps the sandbox (R2 later changed the lines and kept a failed lift). The gate is the step order in a real run's log:
' e2e TestCloneModeLandsTheCloneAndLiftsTheOutputDir reads the rows from its
' own PROVEO_HOME and compares them with a fixed list.
' MEASURED 2026-11-10 on macOS, sbx 0.50.0, the published proveo/claudecode
' image: the test passed at f7ff80a0 (the closure) and on the T3 tree, against
' the same list:
'   agent · credential check · clone mode · pushed branches · egress record ·
'   memory evidence · clone · resume state · home config · remove sandbox
' The other four TestClone* suites passed, one at a time.
' TestHeadedCtrlCAsksBeforeRemoving passed for the three harnesses whose image
' is on the host (claudecode, claudecode-browser, hermes: 9 subtests) and
' skipped the other seven (21 subtests).
'
' ── EACH STEP SAYS IT STARTED, AND ITS CEILING (the rider on T3) ───────────
' Before this the terminal printed a line when a step ENDED, so a slow step
' showed as silence (terminal-handoff-probes.puml, C2). Now `announce(name)`
' prints one line before a step runs:
'   <name> — up to <ceiling>
' A step that starts no subprocess prints its name alone (home config).
' WHICH STEPS PRINT: every top-level step except `clone`, and each of the
' clone's five parts. The parent prints nothing: its parts speak for it.
' THE CEILING IS A SUM, NOT A NEW DEADLINE. stepCeiling(name) adds up the
' bounds the step's calls already have. Nothing new can time out.
'   one `sbx ls`   sbx.ListWait = 10s + 2s grace = 12s
'   one call       guestExecDeadline + guestGrace = 3m2s
'   credential check  ls + the 20s guest read + grace .............. 34s
'   clone mode        the 36s probe + grace ........................ 38s
'   pushed branches   ls + the reflog read ......................... 3m14s
'   egress record     the policy log ............................... 12s
'   memory evidence   ls + the guest read .......................... 24s
'   clone/.env        ls + the read ................................ 3m14s
'   clone/snapshot    one call ..................................... 3m2s
'   clone/output      the lift ..................................... 3m2s
'   clone/branches    ls + remote fetch + tips, bundle, its fetch .. 12m20s
'   clone/spec        ls-files, span, three path lists, restore,
'                     rebase ....................................... 21m14s
'   resume state      ls + the save ................................ 3m14s
'   remove sandbox    one call ..................................... 3m2s
' `pushed branches` counts the part that runs before anything is asked. The
' question and each fetch the operator accepts print their own lines.
' The clone's ref report (two kinds of host git call, one per carried ref)
' sits between two parts and has no line.
' The deadlines are variables, so the ceilings follow a shortened deadline.
' [Unverified] The sums are upper bounds and far above a healthy run. Each
' real-sandbox clone run of 2026-11-10 took 27s to 39s from start to exit,
' teardown included. T2's `step:` rows are the data for tightening them.
' THE LINES ALSO PRINT WHEN A SANDBOX IS RETIRED AT RUN START, because
' PreserveClone serves both. The wording names the step, not "teardown".
' WRITTEN TWICE BY CHOICE: T6 (delivery plan, W5) replaces these lines with a
' live step list that reads the same names and the same stepCeiling.
'
' ── THE RUNNING STEP IS ONE LIVE LINE WITH A CLOCK (T6) ─────────────────────
' The start lines above were written to be replaced. On a terminal a step now
' shows as one line that redraws every second and is erased when the step
' ends:
'   step 6/9 · clone/snapshot · 12s of up to 3m2s
' Its place comes from teardownPlan, its ceiling from stepCeiling, and the
' line itself from ui.Live (_spec/internal/ui/output-vocabulary.puml), so an
' ordinary line a step prints lands above it and the frame is redrawn below.
' A healthy teardown leaves no step lines behind: nine frames replace each
' other and the last one is erased.
' OUTPUT THAT IS NOT A TERMINAL KEEPS THE START LINE (`<name> — up to
' <ceiling>`): a pipe or a CI log cannot redraw.
' THE RUN LOG NOW RECORDS THE START TOO:
'   step: clone/snapshot (up to 3m2s) started
' Before, a row was written when a step ENDED, so a step that never ended
' left none. The start row is what names a hung step in a log.
' The same line shows for the clone's parts when a sandbox is retired at run
' start; outside teardown it carries no `step n/N`.
' A DEVIATION FROM THE DELIVERY PLAN, RECORDED. The plan drew T6 as a list
' inside a Bubble Tea Program. Teardown prints through ui while its steps
' run, holds the terminal in cooked mode for T4's signals, and hands the
' terminal to the pushed-branch dialog in the middle. One live line under
' the Printer's own lock does the job without a second renderer owning the
' terminal; a list would have needed every ui line routed through it.
' THE LOOP MOVED TO ui ON 2026-11-11, AND THE DIALOG STOPPED SHARING THE
' TERMINAL WITH IT. showStep calls ui.Running; init's apply and a run's
' start use the same renderer. MEASURED then: the line drew over the
' pushed-branch question. Every prompt now suspends it:
' _spec/internal/ui/step-lines.puml.
' Pinned by internal/backend/sandbox/teardown_plan_test.go:
'   TestAStepFrameCarriesItsPlaceItsClockAndItsCeiling
'   TestATerminalGetsALiveLinePerStepAndNoStartLines
' and by e2e TestCloneModeLandsTheCloneAndLiftsTheOutputDir: the log holds
' the start row and no frame.
'
' ── A SIGNAL IN TEARDOWN HAS ONE MEANING EACH (T4; D2, D16) ────────────────
' Until T4 a SIGTERM or SIGHUP in teardown was swallowed: forwardStop's parked
' goroutine consumed it and cancelled a context nothing read any more. SIGINT
' had no handler, and the terminal is back in cooked mode by then, so Ctrl+C
' ended proveo on the spot: no carry, no state save, no removal, no line
' (terminal-handoff-probes.puml, C3).
' `teardown.watch` takes SIGINT, SIGTERM and SIGHUP for the length of
' teardown. Run starts it BEFORE it stops forwardStop, so no signal meets a
' gap with Go's default action.
'   SIGINT, first     prints the running step and what leaving keeps:
'                     "⚠ teardown is at <step> — Ctrl+C again leaves now",
'                     dim "leaving keeps sandbox <name>; …" (R2: the ceiling
'                     is the live frame's, not a parenthetical). Nothing
'                     stops.
'   SIGINT, second    ends the context. The call in flight is killed, later
'                     steps are skipped, the sandbox stays, and Run returns
'                     Stopped{SIGINT}: exit 130. The Executor, D2: "Say once,
'                     leave on two".
'   SIGTERM, SIGHUP   the running top-level step finishes. Later steps are
'                     skipped, the sandbox stays, exit 128+signal. D16:
'                     "Finish the step, then leave". A clone in flight is one
'                     step, so the whole carry completes.
'   SIGINT after one  leaves at once and exits 130. The operator was already
'   of those          told teardown is leaving, so Ctrl+C means now.
' A STOP THAT LEAVES NOTHING UNDONE IS NOT A STOP. A SIGTERM during the last
' step lets it finish. Every planned step ran, so the run's own result
' stands. leftBy() names a signal only when a step was skipped or killed.
' WHAT LEAVING PRINTS (leave; R2 below): what came home, then the closing
' kept line and the ide block.
'   ⚠ sb — kept: teardown stopped by SIGINT at remove sandbox
' "not run: <steps>" prints dim when a step never started; a removal the
' Ctrl+C killed did start, so it prints none.
' The home access stays with the kept sandbox (keepHome), as on a failed run.
' THE CARRY STOPS BETWEEN ITS PARTS. After a hard stop PreserveClone returns
' before its next part. Otherwise each remaining call would start, die at
' once, and print a warning of its own.
'
' ── TEARDOWN'S CHILDREN LEAVE THE TERMINAL'S PROCESS GROUP (T4) ─────────────
' A handler alone protects nothing. The terminal sends SIGINT to the whole
' foreground process group, so the `sbx exec` or `git` in flight died on the
' FIRST Ctrl+C (MEASURED with a probe: terminal-handoff-probes.puml, C3).
' ownGroup(cmd) sets Setpgid and makes the command's cancel kill the group.
'   boundCommand          every guest and host call teardown waits on
'   sbx.boundedWith,      the 10s probes behind Running, Exists, the policy
'   boundedCombinedWith   log and memory evidence, and ExecOutput
' ONE CALL STAYS IN THE FOREGROUND: the pushed-branch `git fetch`
' (boundForeground). The operator just asked for it, and git may need the
' terminal for a passphrase. A background group that reads the terminal is
' stopped by SIGTTIN and would sit there until its deadline.
' THE GROUP KILL ALSO REMOVED A WAIT. Before, a deadline killed the direct
' child only; a grandchild holding the output pipe kept the caller for the 2s
' grace. MEASURED on linux/arm64: the package's unit suite went from 24.3s to
' 5.7s.
' [Inference] sbx's bounded calls run outside teardown too. A proveo that dies
' mid-call now leaves that call to end by itself (10s at most) instead of
' taking it down with the terminal's signal. None of them reads the terminal.
' Windows has no process group of this kind; ownGroup does nothing there.
' MEASURED 2026-11-10 on a real terminal (tmux) and a real sandbox, macOS, sbx
' 0.50.0, with `sbx rm` slowed by a PATH shim: the first Ctrl+C printed the
' line and left the removal running; the second exited 130 with the sandbox
' still listed. e2e TestCtrlCInTeardownSaysOnceThenLeavesWith130.
'
' Pinned by internal/backend/sandbox/teardown_signal_test.go:
'   TestTheFirstInterruptSaysWhatLeavingKeepsAndTheSecondLeaves
'   TestATerminateLetsTheRunningStepFinishAndSkipsTheRest
'   TestATerminateDuringTheLastStepLeavesNothingUndone
'   TestAnInterruptAfterATerminateLeavesAtOnce
'   TestAStoppedCarryNamesTheRescueRecipe
'   TestTeardownChildrenRunInTheirOwnProcessGroup
'   TestThePushedFetchStaysInTheTerminalsProcessGroup
' by internal/sbx/proc_unix_test.go:
'   TestABoundedCallRunsInItsOwnProcessGroup
'   TestABoundedCallThatOutlivesItsTimeoutEndsWithItsChildren
' and by e2e/teardown_interrupt_test.go.
'
' Pinned by internal/backend/sandbox/teardown_plan_test.go:
'   TestEveryPlannedStepStartsWithItsNameAndCeilingOnTheTerminal
'   TestStepCeilingsAddUpTheBoundsOfTheCallsAStepMakes
'   TestATeardownRunRecordsExactlyItsPlan
'   TestTheFailedPlanIsTheNormalPlanWithoutTheRemoval
'   TestAPanickingStepBecomesItsErrorAndTheNextStepsStillRun
'   TestAStoppedTeardownSkipsItsStepsAndKeepsTheSandbox
'   TestAStopEndsTheGuestCallInFlight
' and by e2e/teardown_steps_test.go: TestStepRowNameReadsARunLogLine
'
' ── WHAT TEARDOWN PRINTS (R2, 2026-11-11) ──────────────────────────────────
' _spec/_plans/output-readability.puml holds the operator's decisions; this is
' the shape they gave teardown's screen. Two headings at most, each once:
' "results" on every run, "ide" only when the sandbox stays.
'   1. the agent's end, first under results (sandbox.Run, agentEnd):
'        a forwarded SIGTERM/SIGHUP   ● claudecode — stopped by SIGTERM
'        sbx's exit, sbx said nothing × sbx run — exited with code N
'        any other failure            × <the error>; Run returns
'                                     backend.Reported, so main prints
'                                     nothing after the ide block
'        sbx printed its own error, an operator-chosen removal, a clean
'        exit: no line. The run log keeps the outcome either way. A removal
'        the operator chose prints nothing whatever Wait returned: sbx's
'        ExitError, or context.Canceled when sbx exited 0 after the
'        SIGTERM; Run returns that one as backend.Reported (exit 1, no ×).
'   2. live frames (a terminal) or start lines (plain output) per step.
'   3. what came home: ONE sandbox.Block, gathered by the steps and printed
'      when the clone step ends; a failed run's block waits for the state
'      save, then looks up the transcript (see THE TRANSCRIPT FOLLOWS THE
'      STATE SAVE). Block.Print orders its rows by resultOrder, whichever
'      step found a row first (the pushed step runs before the clone's):
'        output · .env · branches · _spec · pushed · transcript
'      then its ⚠ items (credential, expired login, out of memory, .env,
'      _spec, snapshot, lift, carry, fetch). Each step writes into the
'      block, never straight to the screen.
'   4. later steps' ⚠ items as they happen (resume state, home config).
'   5. the closing line, the run's last proveo line unless the sandbox
'      stays:
'        ✓ <name> — removed
'        ⚠ <name> — removed      dim: what did not come home went with it
'                                (a removal the operator chose)
'        ✓ retained engine <name> — holds OpenCode's history
'        ⚠ <name> — kept for diagnosis      (a failed run)
'        ⚠ <name> — kept: <why>  (a stopped teardown, stranded work, a
'                                failed removal)
'        ⚠ <name> — not listed by sbx   (gone after a failed run, a
'                                failed removal, or a stop: no ide block)
'      A kept line carries one dim fix: `proveo rm --stop <name>` while
'      it runs, `proveo rm <name>` once stopped, the rescue line after a
'      removal that timed out. A keep the operator's Ctrl+C alone caused
'      logs its fix instead (decision E); a failed run keeps its fix on
'      screen even when a Ctrl+C ends its teardown, because the agent's
'      failure kept the sandbox. A failed run whose agent drew on the
'      alternate screen (ptyproxy's Proxy.EnteredAltScreen) also gets its
'      last five lines dim.
'   EVERY KEPT LINE ASKS sbx FIRST (R2 review, 2026-11-11). failedEnd and
'   normalEnd's failed removal checked sbx.Exists; leave() did not, so a
'   second Ctrl+C that killed `sbx rm --force` after sbx had deleted the
'   sandbox printed it as kept, with attach facts, and stored a kept entry.
'   leave() now closes such a run on "⚠ <name> — not listed by sbx" with the
'   stop's reason dim, stores no kept entry and still exits 128+signal.
'   ONLY A LISTING THAT NAMES SANDBOXES PROVES ONE GONE (R2 review fix,
'   2026-11-11). sbx.Exists read a header-only `sbx ls` table as "gone", and
'   a busy daemon prints one (auto-memory: sbx ls can return empty). A
'   second Ctrl+C during a slow removal is when the daemon is busiest; a
'   live sandbox then lost its kept entry and homeAccess.Cleanup removed
'   the home access it still mounts. failedEnd, leave() and a failed
'   removal now ask sbx.Gone: the table names other sandboxes and not this
'   one; a header-only table is read again as `sbx ls --json`; an empty
'   listing proves nothing, so the sandbox closes as kept. The same rule
'   as verbs.go trustedListing.
'     NOT CHOSEN: trust an empty listing (drops a live sandbox's home);
'     retry the table (the same busy daemon answers the same way).
'     COST: a run whose sandbox was the host's only one and is gone closes
'     on a kept line; ReconcileResults drops its entry once a listing
'     names any sandbox.
'   THE LEAVE PATH WAITS AT MOST leaveWait (3s) FOR sbx (R3, R2 carry-over
'   1). sbx.Gone and sbx.Running are bounded `sbx ls` calls of up to ~12s
'   each in their own process group, so a third Ctrl+C never reached them
'   and a slow daemon held exit 130 for ~20s. leave() now asks both in one
'   goroutine (fateWithin) and stops waiting at leaveWait: an unanswered
'   listing proves nothing, so the sandbox closes as kept and a failed
'   run's fix names --stop, which `proveo rm` honours for a stopped sandbox
'   too; the run log says sbx did not answer. "Say once, leave on two"
'   holds. NOT CHOSEN: skip the listing on SIGINT (a sandbox sbx finished
'   removing would print as kept); cancel it on a third Ctrl+C (a third
'   keypress to learn of).
'     Pinned by TestASecondCtrlCLeavesWithinItsBoundWhileSbxIsSlowToList.
'   A STRANDED SANDBOX ASKS sbx FIRST TOO (R3, R2 carry-over 2). normalEnd's
'   endKeepStranded asks sbx.Gone like failedEnd, leave() and a failed
'   removal: a sandbox lost during the carry closes on "⚠ <name> — not
'   listed by sbx" with what was stranded dim, and gets no kept entry, no
'   ide block and no kept home access.
'     Pinned by TestAStrandedSandboxSbxNoLongerListsClosesOnTheGoneLine.
'   THE TRANSCRIPT FOLLOWS THE STATE SAVE (R2 review fix, 2026-11-11).
'   R2's first cut looked the transcript up before stepState so the fact
'   could join the block printed after the clone step. The transcript is
'   not on the host yet then: sbx mounts ~/.claude/projects as a
'   per-sandbox volume, and SaveState is what copies it into the proveo
'   home AgentTranscript walks (_spec/internal/sbx/state-sync.puml). So
'   failedEnd runs clone → resume state → transcript lookup → block →
'   home config. A normal run's block still prints after the clone step.
'     NOT CHOSEN: a second block for the transcript (two blocks under one
'     heading, P3); the lookup inside the clone step (nothing copied yet).
'   6. a kept sandbox's "ide" block (ideFacts): ssh, jetbrains, toolbox,
'      vs code, project, with the clone's cautions dim under project.
' The run log keeps what left the screen: the agent's last output, the
' egress-record and memory-log paths, the review and adopt commands, the
' carry's by-hand recipe, the tar recipe, `sbx exec -it <name> bash`.
' A FAILED LIFT STRANDS THE OUTPUT. liftClonedOutput returns "the output is
' only in it", so normalEnd keeps the sandbox; the kept entry records this
' run's output dir (results.Entry.Output, a re-attach's as well as a
' create's) and the launch receipt records the creating run's; `proveo rm`
' lifts into the entry's, else the receipt's (verbs.go keptInput). R3 fixed
' the re-attach: its receipt predates R2 or names the creating run's dir.
' Pinned by TestKeptInputLiftsIntoTheDirTheKeepingRunRecorded and
' TestRemoveKeptLiftsTheOutputTheReceiptRecords (RemoveKept → PreserveClone
' → liftClonedOutput's `tar -cf -`). A stale sandbox's retire keeps it the
' same way (_spec/internal/sbx/clone-workspace.puml).
' THE PLAIN AFTER (MEASURED, probe-plain, NO_COLOR): output that is not a
' terminal prints in step order. The agent's end, then a start line per step
' up to the clone's parts ("credential check — up to 34s" … "clone/spec — up
' to 21m14s"), then the block without marks (".env:", "output:",
' "branches:", "_spec:"), then the start lines of the later steps ("resume
' state — up to 3m14s", "home config", "remove sandbox — up to 3m2s"), then
' "ok: <name> — removed". The block prints when the carry ends (F2), so the
' later steps' start lines follow it. A failed run's block follows the resume
' state's start line (the transcript lookup waits for the state save).
' A RUN THAT NEVER REACHED THE AGENT has no teardown: nothing came home, so
' no "results" heading; main's "× <err>" lands under the last heading drawn:
' the failed step's, except when Run's deferred interface block prints
' first. A failed `sbx create` on a run with interface facts or items prints
' leftByCreate's line under "sbx", then that block under "interface", then
' main's × under "interface" (R2 review, recorded in R3: the block keeps the
' add-ons' warnings a failed run still owes). A failed `sbx create` that left its sandbox listed says
' so first: "⚠ <name> — kept: its create failed", dim "`sbx rm --force
' <name>` removes it" (leftByCreate; no listing proves nothing, so none).
' Pinned by TestAFailedCreateThatLeftASandboxSaysSoAndHowToRemoveIt.
